This folder packages the Rockbox simulator as an Android app: the device
skin, buttons and clickwheel as on the desktop, running on a phone. It is
unrelated to the older Rockbox-as-an-application port in /android.

The APK holds several models, the iPod Video, the iPod mini 2G, the Sansa
Clip Zip and the Toshiba Gigabeat F/X; the user switches between them in
Settings > Android > Model, which restarts the app. Each model's simulator is built as libmain_<model>.so and started by
SDL2's SDLActivity. Codecs and plugins ship in the APK as libraries next to
it, named after the model, because Android won't run code from app storage
(see uisimulator/common/load_code-sim.c). build-apk.sh builds the models
other than the one configured in models/<model> in the build directory.

The simulated disk is the phone's shared storage (/sdcard), so the app asks
for "All files access" on first start. Each model's own files (.rockbox)
are kept in /sdcard/Rockbox/<model>/.rockbox instead of at the root (see
uisimulator/common/filesystem-sim.c), unpacked there on first run and after
updates. The device images are the SVGs in uisimulator/skins.

Plugins > Applications > camera shows the phone's camera live, at the
screen's own size and colours: four dithered greys on the iPod mini, like
a Game Boy Camera, and colour on the others.

    Select          take a photo
    Left / Right    switch between the rear and front cameras
    Wheel, Up/Down  brightness
    Play, Vol, A    on colour screens, switch between colour and a Game Boy
                    mode in the original Game Boy's four greens (iPod
                    Video, Clip Zip, Gigabeat); on the mini, dithering
                    on/off
    Menu, Home      quit

A photo is the picture as it is on the screen, saved as a PNG in
/sdcard/DCIM/Rockbox, scaled up in whole pixels to about 480 high, and
added to the gallery. The app asks for the camera permission the first
time. SimCamera.java runs the camera with Camera2 and hands each frame to
firmware/target/hosted/sdl/camera-sdl.c, which turns it upright and crops
it to the screen for the plugin (apps/plugins/camera.c).

Only arm64 (aarch64) is supported.

* Prerequisites

- Android SDK with build-tools and platforms;android-35
- Android NDK r27 or later
- A JDK
- CMake (or the Android SDK's, "sdkmanager cmake;3.22.1") and curl

configure downloads SDL2 (a pinned release, checked against its SHA-256)
and builds it into the build directory, see build-sdl2.sh. That happens
once; later runs of configure reuse it.

* Build instructions

configure looks for the SDK in $ANDROID_HOME, $ANDROID_SDK_ROOT and
~/Android/Sdk, and uses the newest NDK in it. Elsewhere, or to pick an NDK:

    export ANDROID_SDK_PATH=~/Android/Sdk
    export ANDROID_NDK_PATH=~/Android/Sdk/ndk/<version>

Then:

    mkdir build-android && cd build-android
    ../tools/configure --target=androidsim

(or run configure without options and pick 202, "Android iPod sim")
    make && make install
    ../uisimulator/android/build-apk.sh
    adb install -r rockbox-sim.apk

* The iPod mini 2G in 3D

Target 203, "Android mini 3D" (configure --target=androidsim3d, in a
build dir of its own), makes a separate app, Rockbox 3D (org.rockbox.sim3d),
with just the iPod mini 2G, drawn as a 3D model with OpenGL ES 3
(firmware/target/hosted/sdl/window-3d.c): the screen shows on its display,
the wheel and buttons work where they are, the hold switch slides, and
dragging turns it, pinching zooms. Settings > Android > Gyroscope keeps it
still as the phone turns, and the dock cable slides in when the phone
charges.

The model isn't in the source: build-apk.sh makes it with
uisimulator/skins/ipodmini2g-3d.py (numpy and Pillow) from these zips in
the source dir, or where MINI3D_MODEL, MINI3D_OBJ and MINI3D_CABLE say:

    945950-gltf-extended.gltf.zip      the iPod's materials and textures
    iPod_Mini_Blue_obj.zip             its shape, subdivided as its author did
    30-pin-connector-to-usb-cable-1-meter-3-feet-1.snapshot.5.zip
                                       the cable, which can be left out

Without the first two, the app has the 2D skin.

The simulator's output is written to stdout.txt in each model's dir in the
app's private files:

    adb shell run-as org.rockbox.sim cat files/<model>/stdout.txt

The APK is signed with the debug key (~/.android/debug.keystore) unless
ANDROID_KEYSTORE names another keystore, with its password in
ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASS in apksigner's form (pass:, file: or env:), as for
a release:

    ANDROID_KEYSTORE=~/.android/rockbox-release.jks \
    ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASS=file:$HOME/.android/rockbox-release.pass \
        ../uisimulator/android/build-apk.sh

Android only updates an app with one signed by the same key, so going
between debug and release builds needs an uninstall in between.

To see where the buttons react to taps, build the APK with
SIM_DEBUG_BUTTONS=1 build-apk.sh: it outlines them (--debugbuttons), and
stdout.txt shows which button each tap hit.
